<linker>
  <!--
    link.xml — IL2CPP managed-code preservation rules for the UberStrike mobile build.

    Reasoning:
      * ProjectSettings.asset has `stripEngineCode: 1` and `managedStrippingLevel` of 1 (Low)
        across all build platforms. The Unity linker walks roots (scenes, build settings,
        [Preserve] attributes, link.xml) and prunes anything else.
      * UberStrike runtime patterns that the linker CANNOT see through:
          1. `Singleton<T>` and `RenderSettingsController` use
             `GetConstructor(BindingFlags.NonPublic)` to invoke private constructors on
             concrete subclass types. The linker sees `T` only as a generic parameter,
             not as the 35+ concrete subclasses (BackgroundMusicPlayer, ChatManager,
             AmmoHud, etc.). Cmune's 4.3.10.1 iOS build relied on the same pattern.
          2. Prime31 StoreKit / Etcetera native plugins invoke managed callbacks via
             `UnitySendMessage("StoreKitManager", "productPurchased", json)` —
             dispatched by GameObject name + method-name string, invisible to the linker.
          3. Photon / UberStrike.UnitySdk perform serialization via reflection.

    AOT (IL2CPP) note — runtime generic construction:
      The mobile JIT/AOT audit (2026-05-13/14) decompiled all three plugin DLLs.
      The ONLY genuine AOT-fragile site is Photon3Unity3D's
      `Protocol.DeserializeDictionary`, which does
        `typeof(Dictionary<,>).MakeGenericType(k, v)` + `Activator.CreateInstance`
      over value-type keys/values returned by `Protocol.GetTypeOfCode`. link.xml
      preservation alone does NOT fix this — IL2CPP must have *generated* each
      `Dictionary<K,V>` instantiation at compile time. That is handled by
      `Assets/Scripts/AOT/PhotonAotHints.cs` (statically references every value-type
      combo), which lives inside Assembly-CSharp and is covered by the rule below.
      P31RestKit's `MakeGenericMethod` sites (`toClass<T>`/`toList<T>`) are DEAD CODE
      in this project — grep finds zero `[P31DeserializeableField]` usages across
      `Assets/` — so they need no AOT hint; the preserve rule below is belt-and-braces.

    First-cut scope:
      * Preserve all of Assembly-CSharp ("preserve all" — broad but safe). Stripping
        savings on this assembly are modest compared to engine code stripping, which
        remains active.
      * Preserve UberStrike.UnitySdk and ExitGames.Client.Photon types referenced via
        reflection by network serialization.
      * Tighten by switching from `preserve="all"` to enumerated `<type>` rules once a
        green IL2CPP build verifies which symbols are actually needed.
  -->

  <assembly fullname="Assembly-CSharp" preserve="all" />

  <!-- Photon transport — held alive by string-based event registration -->
  <assembly fullname="Photon3Unity3D" preserve="all" />

  <!-- UberStrike SDK assembly — reflection-based view serialization -->
  <assembly fullname="UberStrike.UnitySdk" preserve="all" />

  <!--
    Prime31 native plugins:
      P31RestKit handles HTTP callbacks via UnitySendMessage. Its reflection-based
      `P31DeserializationExtensions.toClass<T>/toList<T>` (the `MakeGenericMethod`
      sites) are dead code here — no `[P31DeserializeableField]` attributes exist in
      `Assets/`. Preserve fully anyway until the green-build pass confirms which entry
      points are reached; the assembly is only 16 KB.
  -->
  <assembly fullname="P31RestKit" preserve="all" />

  <!-- System.Core: LINQ extension methods are referenced from generic instantiations
       across UberStrike. Without this, IL2CPP may drop Enumerable.Select<int,bool>
       etc. specializations that are referenced only via lambdas. -->
  <assembly fullname="System.Core">
    <type fullname="System.Linq.Enumerable" preserve="all" />
  </assembly>

</linker>
